Build prompt: TYPE OR DIE

Paste everything below into a fresh conversation as a single request.

Build a single-file browser game called TYPE OR DIE — a first-person typing survival horror game. Deliver one self-contained .html file (Three.js r128 via cdnjs, procedural Web Audio, vanilla JS in an IIFE, no build step, no external image/sound/font assets besides Google Fonts Creepster + Special Elite). The player kills approaching creatures by typing the word floating above each one's head; every correct letter fires a shot, and finishing the word kills it.

Before writing any code, plan the full architecture below — camera/aim math, entity system, environment system — since these are load-bearing and hard to retrofit.

Core fantasy

Graveyard-horror typing shooter. The player stands in one spot (no WASD movement) while creatures close in from every direction, including from behind. Survival depends on typing speed, accuracy, and target prioritization, not movement.

Camera & auto-aim (the single most important system)

Enemies spawn at a full 360° random angle around the player, at a fixed eye height of 1.6. Because the player can't turn manually in a satisfying way while typing, the camera must automatically aim itself at whatever the player is currently typing. This is not optional polish — it's the mechanic that makes 360° spawning fair and readable. Get this exactly right:

Track a smoothed camYaw/camPitch. Each frame, compute a desired yaw/pitch:

If there's an active (locked) target: get its head's world position (tx, ty, tz). With the player fixed at the origin:

desiredYaw = atan2(-tx, -tz) — this is the correct sign convention for a camera whose default forward is -Z in Three.js. Verify this empirically (screenshot a target off to one side, confirm it centers under the crosshair) rather than trusting derivation alone.

planarDist = hypot(tx, tz); desiredPitch = clamp(atan2(ty - 1.6, planarDist), -0.5, 0.5).

If nothing is locked: idle behavior — a slow sine-wave scan (±0.25 rad) plus a small mouse-parallax offset, so the view feels alive without being disorienting.

Interpolate toward the desired angle every frame with a frame-rate-independent lerp: angle += (desired - angle) * min(delta * lerpSpeed, 1), using a fast lerpSpeed (~7) while locked and a slow one (~2.5) while idle.

Improvement over a naive implementation: because targets can be almost directly behind the player, a plain linear lerp on raw yaw values can occasionally take the long way around near the ±π boundary. Use a wraparound-safe angle interpolation:

function angleLerp(current, target, t) {
  let diff = ((target - current + Math.PI) % (Math.PI*2) + Math.PI*2) % (Math.PI*2) - Math.PI;
  return current + diff * t;
}

Layer on top: idle head-bob, mouse parallax (small, secondary — auto-aim dominates while locked), and screen shake on hits/damage (decaying magnitude over ~0.4s).

Attach a spotlight "flashlight" to the camera so whatever you're aiming at is lit.

Test this explicitly: type a letter that locks a creature spawned well off to the side or behind the player, wait for the lerp to settle, and confirm via screenshot that it's centered under the crosshair.

Targeting & typing logic

Each enemy/pickup has a word (or a queue of words/phrases for multi-stage kills — see Bosses) and a typedIndex.

On keydown: if there's a valid active target, check the next expected character; correct → advance typedIndex, wrong → miss (break combo). If there's no active target, search all alive enemies + pickups whose word starts with the typed letter, pick the nearest, lock it, and count that keystroke as its first correct letter.

Word completion kills the enemy (or advances to the next phase for multi-word entities).

Track combo count (breaks on miss), a combo-based score multiplier, WPM, and accuracy, all shown live in the HUD.

There must be a way to prevent random-guessing if enemies span outside of the user's field of view.

The aiming must be pointed to the enemy being attacked, for immersion.

Threat radar

A small circular canvas HUD element (not part of the 3D scene) in a bottom corner:

World-fixed dot plot of every alive enemy (color-coded per type) and pickup (white-ringed), positioned by actual world (x, z) relative to the player, clamped to the radar's radius so distant threats show at the edge.

A rotating triangle at the center showing the camera's current facing direction (use the negative of camera yaw when rotating the canvas, and verify the triangle actually points at a dot you're locked onto).

This is what makes 360° spawning fair: the player always has pre-aim situational awareness even before locking on.

