Bosses

Every 5th wave, spawn one boss with a 3-phrase kill sequence (each phrase includes spaces). Track queueIndex generically (the same multi-word system used for the brute's 2-word sequence) so there's one code path for "entities with more than one word to kill." Show a name + health bar HUD element (health = 100 - queueIndex*phaseSize - (typedIndex/word.length)*phaseSize), tinted to that boss's accent color. Boss stops approaching at a stand-off distance and periodically deals ranged damage instead.

Environments that shift between waves

Define 5 environment themes (e.g. graveyard, hollow forest, sunken crypt, burning village, rotting swamp), each specifying: fog color/density, ambient/moonlight/torch colors and intensities, a procedurally generated ground texture (canvas noise, two-tone), a procedurally generated sky gradient (canvas texture on a large inverted sphere), and a decoration type (tombstones / dead trees / broken pillars / charred ruins / reeds) built from primitives and scattered in a full circle around the player.

Advance to the next theme every wave, with a ~1s fade-to-black transition swapping the scene at the midpoint. Show the theme name as a subtitle under the wave banner. This is what keeps repeated waves from feeling monotonous — the place changes, not just the numbers.

Dispose old textures/materials when swapping themes (use a recursive object.traverse() disposal helper) so a long session doesn't leak GPU memory across dozens of theme swaps.

Visual polish checklist

Procedural starfield (sprites on a large sphere, gentle opacity twinkle).

Ground blood/slime decals at kill locations: a flat, irregular splat texture (procedural canvas, several overlapping radial gradients), tinted per-creature-color, capped at ~25–30 concurrent (oldest removed), fading over several seconds.

Particle bursts on hit (small) and kill (larger, boss/brute bigger still), additive-blended glow sprites with gravity.

Line tracers from camera to target on every correct keystroke.

A signature first-person prop: a low-poly antique typewriter attached to the camera, tucked into a bottom corner, with a key that visibly bounces on every correct keystroke and a small "carriage kick" on word completion. Placement warning learned the hard way: small objects placed too close to the camera in a shared-FOV perspective view appear enormous — a ~0.5-unit-wide object 0.7 units from the camera can fill the entire screen. Scale the whole prop down (~0.4–0.5x) and/or push it further out (~0.85–1.0 units), position it off-center toward a corner, and screenshot-verify it doesn't obscure the crosshair or center of the screen before moving on.

Screen vignette, hit-flash, lightning flashes (periodic + on boss-phase transitions), low-health heartbeat pulse (interval shortens as health drops).

Power-ups

Rare glowing pickups (glowing icosahedron + slowly rotating ring), max one on screen at a time, spawning every ~25–45s, despawning if not collected within ~8s. Typed exactly like an enemy (share the same word/typedIndex/label-sprite shape so targeting code doesn't need a special case). At least: heal, nuke (kill all non-boss enemies on screen), freeze (slow all enemies to a crawl for ~5s), rapid (double score multiplier for ~10s). Show active buff timers as small HUD badges, plus a faint full-screen color tint while each is active.

HUD

Health bar + wave/kills/score/combo (top-left), WPM/accuracy (top-right), wave banner with theme subtitle (center, on wave change), boss name + health bar (top-center, boss fights only), target word with typed/untyped styling and a lock-color border matching the current target's signature color (bottom-center), a plain crosshair that recolors when locked, the radar (bottom corner), and transient floating popups for score gains, "PERFECT" (zero-miss kill bonus), and combo milestones (e.g. every 10th kill).

Audio

Fully procedural Web Audio (oscillators + filtered noise buffers) — no audio files. Cover: shoot/clack, hit, kill, miss, hurt, heartbeat, wave-start, word-complete "ding," pickup chime, nuke boom, boss roar/phase-change/attack, background effects/music, ambient low drone + filtered noise bed, and periodic thunder.

Difficulty curve

Scale spawn rate, max concurrent enemies, enemy speed, word length, and the chance of generating gibberish "words" (vs. real dictionary words) with wave number, with sane caps so late waves stay hard but not impossible. Increase the boss/brute pool and kills-required-per-wave gradually.

Suggested improvements beyond a first pass

Consider a light meta-progression: every N waves survived unlocks a small permanent perk choice (e.g. wider auto-aim assist window, faster combo decay forgiveness) to add a session-to-session goal.

Track and display max combo and per-run creature-kill breakdown on the game-over screen, not just totals.

Keep the radar's per-type color coding but consider distinguishing dot shape too (circle vs. triangle vs. square) for colorblind accessibility, in addition to color.

QA process (do this before considering it done)

Extract the <script> contents and run node --check on it after every meaningful edit.

Grep for duplicate HTML ids and verify tag balance (div/script/style/button open vs. close counts).

Run headless Puppeteer smoke tests (Chromium with --no-sandbox --use-gl=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader --ignore-gpu-blocklist, since sandboxed environments often lack a real GPU) capturing pageerror and console events, with generous settle time (software rendering is slow — wait 1.5–3s after state changes before screenshotting).

Specifically verify, with real keyboard-driven interaction (not just direct state injection, which can bypass the render loop's natural timing): the start screen, that typing a letter locks the nearest matching target and the camera visibly turns to face it, that a full wave-kill cycle advances to the next wave/environment without errors, that a boss fight's multi-phrase sequence and health bar work, that a pickup can be collected, and that the game-over screen renders correctly on death.

For isolated visual checks of individual creature types/themes/bosses, it's reasonable to temporarily expose key internals (applyEnvironment, spawnBoss, spawnPickup, createEnemyInstance, scene, STATE) on a throwaway debug copy of the file — never ship that hook in the final file.

Deliverable

One .html file, saved to the outputs directory and presented to the user. No separate CSS/JS files, no external assets beyond the two Google Fonts and the Three.js CDN script tag.
